__d(function(e,a,n,i,t,r,s){t.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:6,chapter_title:"Changes Around Us",estimated_study_time_minutes:40,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Distinguish between reversible and irreversible changes","Identify and classify everyday changes as reversible or irreversible with reasons","Understand the concept of expansion and contraction due to heat","Explain how dissolving, condensation, and physical state changes relate to reversibility","Connect the concept of irreversibility to burning, cooking, and chemical changes","Use correct scientific vocabulary: reversible, irreversible, expansion, contraction"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Awareness that things around us change \u2014 water becomes ice, wood burns, milk turns to curd","Basic understanding of heating and cooling from daily experience","Familiarity with materials from Chapter 4 (materials and their properties)"],introduction:{why_important:"Everything around us is changing constantly \u2014 ice melts, iron rusts, wood burns, dough becomes bread. Knowing which changes can be undone and which cannot is fundamental to science, engineering, cooking, and environmental understanding.",real_life_relevance:"When you fold paper, you can unfold it \u2014 but once you tear it, you cannot make it whole again. When you melt butter, you can cool it back \u2014 but once you fry something in it, the fried food cannot become raw again. Reversibility is all around us.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka's volcanic Deccan Plateau rock formed through irreversible cooling of lava millions of years ago. The famous Mysuru silk weaving \u2014 once the silk is cut and sewn into a saree, the process is irreversible. Traditional Karnataka pottery (from Dharwad, Bidar, Molakalmuru) involves firing clay \u2014 an irreversible change. Reversible changes appear in Karnataka's summer evaporation of Bellandur lake and seasonal flooding of paddy fields.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 4 introduced materials and their properties. Chapter 5 covered physical separation. This chapter examines what happens when materials change \u2014 and whether those changes can be undone."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Reversible Changes",role:"Changes that can be undone \u2014 the original substance can be recovered"},{name:"Irreversible Changes",role:"Changes that cannot be undone \u2014 the original substance cannot be recovered"},{name:"Heat and Expansion",role:"Heating causes most materials to expand; cooling causes them to contract \u2014 both usually reversible"}],plot:["The chapter opens with the observation that changes happen everywhere and all the time.","It introduces the key question: can every change be reversed?","Reversible changes are explored \u2014 melting of ice, dissolving of salt in water, stretching a rubber band, folding paper.","Irreversible changes are explored \u2014 burning of paper, cooking an egg, souring of milk, breaking a glass.","The concept of expansion (materials getting bigger when heated) is introduced with examples.","Contraction (materials getting smaller when cooled) is paired with expansion.","The chapter explains why railways have gaps in tracks (thermal expansion), and how tight jar lids can be loosened by heating.","It distinguishes between physical changes (usually reversible) and chemical changes (usually irreversible).","Students are encouraged to observe changes at home and classify them."],moral:"Not all changes can be undone. Understanding which changes are reversible and which are permanent helps us make better decisions in science, cooking, and everyday life."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Reversible Changes",simple_explanation:"A reversible change is one where you can get the original substance back. The change can be undone.",detailed_explanation:"A reversible change does not produce a new substance \u2014 the original substance can be recovered. Examples: (1) Melting of ice \u2014 ice melts to water (heating), and water freezes back to ice (cooling). The substance is still water/H\u2082O throughout. (2) Dissolving salt in water \u2014 the salt dissolves and seems to disappear, but it can be recovered by evaporating the water. (3) Stretching a rubber band \u2014 it returns to original shape when released. (4) Folding paper \u2014 can be unfolded. (5) Melting of wax \u2014 wax candle melts when heated, solidifies when cooled. (6) Blowing up a balloon \u2014 can be deflated. Reversible changes are usually physical changes \u2014 they involve a change in shape, size, or state, but not chemical composition.",real_life_example:"When you heat butter, it melts into liquid. When you cool it, it solidifies again. The butter is unchanged chemically \u2014 this is a reversible change. Similarly, when you dissolve sugar in water for lemonade, the sugar hasn't been destroyed \u2014 you could evaporate the water and get the sugar back.",karnataka_example:"During Karnataka's hot summer, water from lakes like Lalbagh Lake in Bengaluru evaporates (reversible \u2014 clouds form and rain brings the water back). The seasonal floods in the Cauvery delta represent the reversible water cycle.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think dissolving is irreversible because you cannot see the dissolved substance. It IS reversible \u2014 you can recover the dissolved substance by evaporation. However, if the dissolution causes a chemical reaction (like dissolving a tablet that fizzes), that may be irreversible.",memory_tip:"REVERSIBLE = can REVERSE = can go BACK. Melt \u2192 freeze back. Dissolve \u2192 evaporate back. Stretch \u2192 release back."},{concept_name:"Irreversible Changes",simple_explanation:"An irreversible change is one where you CANNOT get the original substance back. A new substance is usually formed.",detailed_explanation:"An irreversible change produces a new substance with different properties. The original cannot be recovered. Examples: (1) Burning of paper \u2014 paper burns to ash and smoke. You cannot get paper back from ash. (2) Cooking an egg \u2014 raw egg proteins change permanently when cooked. Cooked egg cannot become raw again. (3) Souring of milk \u2014 milk turns sour (curd) due to bacterial action. You cannot get fresh milk back from curd. (4) Rusting of iron \u2014 iron combines with oxygen and moisture to form rust (iron oxide). Rust \u2260 iron. (5) Burning of wood \u2014 wood turns to ash, charcoal, and gases. Cannot be reversed. (6) Setting of cement \u2014 liquid cement sets into hard solid permanently. Irreversible changes involve chemical reactions \u2014 new substances with different properties are formed.",real_life_example:"When you cook a dosa batter on a hot tawa, the liquid batter turns into a crispy dosa \u2014 you cannot turn the dosa back into liquid batter. When wood burns in your chulha, the ash that remains is not wood \u2014 it's a new substance. Both are irreversible.",karnataka_example:"Traditional Bidar craft (Bidriware) involves irreversible changes \u2014 zinc and copper alloy is cast into shapes and blackened using a special soil process. Once made, the shape and composition cannot be reversed. Karnataka's iron ore smelting in Bellary and iron-related industries involve irreversible chemical changes.",common_mistakes:"Students confuse 'cannot easily undo' with 'irreversible'. Irreversible means you CANNOT get the original back AT ALL \u2014 not just that it is difficult. Also, some students think all cooking is reversible \u2014 cooking is irreversible because chemical bonds in proteins and starches change permanently.",memory_tip:"IRREVERSIBLE = IR = It's REALLY final. Burn, Cook, Rust, Rot, Set cement \u2014 all irreversible. Remember the BCRRS rule."},{concept_name:"Expansion and Contraction",simple_explanation:"When things are heated, they usually expand (get bigger). When cooled, they contract (get smaller). This is almost always reversible.",detailed_explanation:"Thermal expansion: most substances (solids, liquids, and gases) expand when heated because their particles vibrate more and need more space. Thermal contraction: when cooled, particles slow down and the substance shrinks. This is usually reversible \u2014 heat it up, it expands; cool it down, it contracts. Practical applications: (1) Railway tracks have gaps at joints to allow expansion in summer heat \u2014 without gaps, tracks would buckle. (2) Electrical wires hang loosely between poles for the same reason (they contract in winter). (3) A tight metal jar lid can be loosened by running hot water over it \u2014 the metal expands, loosening the grip. (4) Thermometers work because mercury or alcohol expands when temperature rises. (5) Balloon expands when heated; shrinks when cooled.",real_life_example:"If you've ever had trouble opening a glass jar with a metal lid, running the lid under hot water for 30 seconds usually works \u2014 the metal lid expands slightly more than the glass, loosening the seal. This is thermal expansion in action.",karnataka_example:"The railway lines on the Konkan Railway (passing through coastal Karnataka) have expansion joints at regular intervals to accommodate the significant temperature variation between the cool monsoon season and the hot summer \u2014 without these, tracks would buckle in the heat.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think expansion is irreversible. It is NOT \u2014 cooling the expanded material will contract it back. However, if heating causes a chemical change (like burning), that's irreversible \u2014 the expansion was just the physical part. Also, water is unusual \u2014 it EXPANDS when it freezes (solidifies), which is the exception to the general rule.",memory_tip:"HEAT \u2192 EXPAND (getting Excited and spreading out). COOL \u2192 CONTRACT (getting Cold and Curling inward). HC \u2192 EC: Heat/Contract... wait \u2014 HE+CC: Heat-Expands, Cold-Contracts."}],key_vocabulary:[{term:"Change",definition:"Any alteration in the physical or chemical properties of a substance."},{term:"Reversible change",definition:"A change that can be undone and the original substance can be recovered. Usually a physical change."},{term:"Irreversible change",definition:"A change that cannot be undone; a new substance with different properties is formed. Usually a chemical change."},{term:"Expansion",definition:"An increase in the size or volume of a substance, usually caused by heating."},{term:"Contraction",definition:"A decrease in the size or volume of a substance, usually caused by cooling."},{term:"Physical change",definition:"A change in the shape, size, or state of a substance without changing its chemical composition. Usually reversible."},{term:"Chemical change",definition:"A change that produces a new substance with different properties. Usually irreversible."}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Reversible Changes",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Is melting of ice a reversible or irreversible change? Give a reason.",a:"Reversible. Melted ice (water) can be frozen again to get ice. No new substance is formed \u2014 it is still water/H\u2082O throughout. It is a change of state, not a chemical change."},{type:"Application",q:"Salt dissolves in water and you cannot see it. Is this a reversible change? How can you prove it?",a:"Yes, it is reversible. You can recover the salt by evaporating the water \u2014 the salt will reappear as crystals. The salt was never destroyed \u2014 it was only dispersed into the water."}]},{after_concept:"Irreversible Changes",questions:[{type:"Understanding",q:"Why is burning of paper an irreversible change?",a:"When paper burns, it is chemically transformed into ash, water vapour, carbon dioxide, and smoke. These are all new substances with completely different properties. You cannot take ash and turn it back into paper."},{type:"Application",q:"Is souring of milk reversible or irreversible? Explain.",a:"Irreversible. When milk sours, bacteria chemically convert lactose (milk sugar) into lactic acid, permanently changing the composition. You cannot turn sour curd back into fresh milk."}]},{after_concept:"Expansion and Contraction",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"Why do railway tracks have small gaps at the joints between rails?",a:"Railway tracks (metal) expand when heated in summer. If there were no gaps, the expanding rails would push against each other and buckle (bend and warp). The gaps provide space for the metal to expand without damaging the tracks."}]}],key_takeaways:["A reversible change can be undone \u2014 the original substance can be recovered.","An irreversible change cannot be undone \u2014 a new substance is formed.","Physical changes (melting, dissolving, stretching) are usually reversible.","Chemical changes (burning, cooking, rusting) are usually irreversible.","Heating causes most substances to expand; cooling causes them to contract.","Expansion and contraction are reversible physical changes.","Railway tracks have expansion joints to prevent buckling in heat.","Dissolving is reversible (evaporate to recover the dissolved substance).","Cooking an egg, burning paper, souring of milk are all irreversible.","Karnataka examples: Bidar craft (irreversible), railway expansion joints on Konkan Railway (expansion), water cycle (reversible)."],mind_map:{root:"Changes Around Us",branches:[{branch:"Reversible Changes",sub:["Melting of ice","Dissolving of salt","Stretching rubber band","Folding paper","Melting wax"]},{branch:"Irreversible Changes",sub:["Burning of paper/wood","Cooking food","Souring of milk","Rusting of iron","Setting of cement"]},{branch:"Expansion and Contraction",sub:["Heat \u2192 Expansion","Cooling \u2192 Contraction","Railway track gaps","Tight jar lid \u2014 heat to open","Thermometer works on expansion"]},{branch:"Physical vs Chemical Change",sub:["Physical = shape/size/state change, usually reversible","Chemical = new substance formed, usually irreversible"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Bidriware craft \u2014 irreversible","Konkan Railway expansion joints","Water cycle \u2014 reversible","Clay pottery firing \u2014 irreversible"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 6 distinguishes between reversible and irreversible changes. Reversible: original substance can be recovered, usually physical changes \u2014 melting ice (water \u2192 ice \u2192 water), dissolving salt in water (recover by evaporation), stretching rubber band, folding paper, melting wax. Irreversible: new substance formed, cannot undo \u2014 burning paper (ash/smoke formed), cooking egg (proteins denature), souring milk (chemical change by bacteria), rusting iron (new compound \u2014 iron oxide), setting cement. Expansion and contraction: most substances expand when heated and contract when cooled (reversible). This principle explains: railway track expansion joints, loose electrical wires, opening tight jar lids with hot water, thermometers. Water is exceptional \u2014 it expands when frozen. Karnataka: Bidriware/pottery firing are irreversible; Konkan Railway expansion joints; monsoon water cycle is reversible.",must_remember_facts:["Reversible change = can undo. Irreversible change = cannot undo (new substance formed).","Melting ice, dissolving salt, stretching rubber band = REVERSIBLE.","Burning paper/wood, cooking egg, souring milk, rusting iron = IRREVERSIBLE.","Physical changes are usually reversible. Chemical changes are usually irreversible.","Heat \u2192 Expansion. Cold \u2192 Contraction. (Exception: water expands on freezing.)","Railway tracks have gaps to allow expansion in summer \u2014 without gaps, tracks buckle.","Dissolving is reversible \u2014 evaporate the water and get the dissolved substance back.","Cooking is irreversible \u2014 cooked food cannot become raw again."]}},2530,[]);